olars in mali from the 11th  to the 20th  century more than 400 000 of them are  temporarily stored in this library  the writings cover a broad range of  subjects including arts medicine science  astrology mathematics and politics  everybody says that in africa there is  no written heritage  there is no written history that it's  all oral  but the manuscripts are our identity  they are our history  they are our heritage  really the manuscripts are everything to  us  for decades dr haidera has worked to  preserve and draw attention to these  manuscripts  in 2012 jihadists attacked northern mali  and attempted to destroy historical  buildings and burn the manuscripts  but with the help of locals hydara  secretly transported the manuscripts a  bit at a time by boat and truck to the  capital bamaku  now together with his team and partners  like google hydera has been working to  digitize the manuscripts  they've taken thousands of  high-definition pictures of the  manuscripts and translated the text into  different languages for people all over  the world to use  gang honor  having a digital copy of the manuscripts  will make work easier for researchers  for students  for teachers  and for historians  they'll be able to easily access the  manuscripts on their screens  this particular manuscript that i just  read is titled good governance and the  dangers of power and basically talks  about how giving gifts and money in  exchange for justice is corruption and i  find it's very very fascinating that  from the 11th century we can find so  much wisdom that people and governments  of our day can learn from  digitizing the manuscripts also means  that even if any of them gets lost or  stolen the content will be preserved  online  this digitization process is part of  google's mali magic project  the technology giant is partnering with  historians like hydara to preserve and  share treasures of african heritage with  the world  you
 

